  "live_records": {
    "q-340855": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340855",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
